{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"Q9ZFU7","uniProtkbId":"EUTR_SALTY","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"2000-05-30","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2026-09-02","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"1999-05-01","entryVersion":135,"sequenceVersion":1},"annotationScore":3.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Salmonella typhimurium (strain LT2 / SGSC1412 / ATCC 700720)","taxonId":99287,"lineage":["Bacteria","Pseudomonadati","Pseudomonadota","Gammaproteobacteria","Enterobacterales","Enterobacteriaceae","Salmonella"]},"proteinExistence":"1: Evidence at protein level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"HTH-type DNA-binding transcriptional activator EutR"}},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"value":"Ethanolamine operon regulatory protein"}}]},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"2656649"}],"value":"eutR"},"orderedLocusNames":[{"value":"STM2454"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"1328159"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"26565973"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8113167"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"1328159"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"2656649"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"8113167"}],"value":"Activates the transcription of the eut operon, allowing utilization of ethanolamine (EA). Positively regulates its own transcription (Probable) (PubMed:1328159, PubMed:8113167). Probably binds EA and vitamin B12 as effectors (Probable). Competes with ethanolamine ammonia-lysase (EAL, the first enzyme in the EA degradation pathway) for adenosylcobalamin (PubMed:8113167). Ethanolamine-associated signaling mediated via this protein, but not EA degradation, impacts S.typhimurium survival within macrophages. Binds the promoter of ssrB and eutS in vitro; in mouse infection models binding to ssrB probably induces all 4 operons of pathogenicity island SPI-2 (PubMed:26565973)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"29531136"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"}],"value":"Expression of the eut operon allows this bacteria to use ethanolamine (EA) as a carbon, nitrogen and energy source. It relies on cobalamin (vitamin B12) both as a cofactor for the ethanolamine ammonia-lyase (EAL) activity and to induce the operon (PubMed:3045078). EA enhances bacterial survival in macrophages in a concentration-dependent manner, suggesting it is an important nutrient in infection (PubMed:29531136)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"}],"value":"Amine and polyamine degradation; ethanolamine degradation"}],"commentType":"PATHWAY"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10464203"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"1328159"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"26565973"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"2656649"}],"value":"Part of the 17-gene eut operon transcribed from a single promoter, positively regulates its own expression, induced by ethanolamine and adenosylcobalamin (AdoCbl, vitamin B12). This is the last gene in the operon and has a second weaker promoter that is constitutively transcribed at a low level (Probable) (PubMed:10464203, PubMed:1328159, PubMed:26565973, PubMed:3045078). Subject to catabolite repression; expression is substantially lower during growth on glucose than during growth versus succinate. Catabolite repression is overcome by adding exogenous cAMP to the glucose growth media (PubMed:1328159). Induced after phagocytosis by mouse RAW macrophages and in spleen in mouse infection; the whole eut operon is not induced (PubMed:26565973)"}],"commentType":"INDUCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"2656649"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"26684793"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"29531136"}],"value":"Mutations prevent expression of the entire eut operon (PubMed:2656649). No phenotype during bacterial growth in vitro. About 10-fold outcompeted by wild-type in mouse intestines at 2 and 4 days following oral infection. Loss of induction of pathogenicity island SPI-2 in mouse macrophages (PubMed:26684793). About 25% reduction in survival in mouse macrophage assays. Bacteria growth is not enhanced by exogenous EA in macrophage survival assays (PubMed:29531136)"}],"commentType":"DISRUPTION PHENOTYPE"}],"features":[{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":350,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"HTH-type DNA-binding transcriptional activator EutR","featureId":"PRO_0000194510"},{"type":"Domain","location":{"start":{"value":243,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":344,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"HTH araC/xylS-type","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"PROSITE-ProRule","id":"PRU00593"}]},{"type":"DNA binding","location":{"start":{"value":260,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":281,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"H-T-H motif","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"PROSITE-ProRule","id":"PRU00593"}]},{"type":"DNA binding","location":{"start":{"value":311,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":334,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"H-T-H motif","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"PROSITE-ProRule","id":"PRU00593"}]}],"keywords":[{"id":"KW-0010","category":"Molecular function","name":"Activator"},{"id":"KW-0238","category":"Molecular function","name":"DNA-binding"},{"id":"KW-1185","category":"Technical term","name":"Reference proteome"},{"id":"KW-0804","category":"Biological process","name":"Transcription"},{"i